{"tool_name":"geneimprint","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"geneimprint","home":"http://www.geneimprint.com/site/genes-by-species","summary":"Catalogue of genes (from different species including human) that contains general information about a gene as well as whether it is imprinted and which allele is expressed.","addition_date":"2017-09-26T07:54:40Z","last_update_date":"2020-06-16T10:55:23Z","tool_type":["Database portal","Web application"],"collections":["Rare Disease"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:geneimprint","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"jirtle@geneimprint.com","name":"Dr. Randy L. Jirtle","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"http://www.geneimprint.com/site/about-this-site#contact"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Deposition","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3431"},{"term":"Gene expression analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2495"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"Geneimprint","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"ELIXIR-EE","publication":[{"doi":"10.1016/S0002-9440(10)65309-6","metadata":{"abstract":"Genomic imprinting refers to an epigenetic marking of genes that results in monoallelic expression. This parent-of-origin dependent phenomenon is a notable exception to the laws of Mendelian genetics. Imprinted genes are intricately involved in fetal and behavioral development. Consequently, abnormal expression of these genes results in numerous human genetic disorders including carcinogenesis. This paper reviews genomic imprinting and its role in human disease. Additional information about imprinted genes can be found on the Genomic Imprinting Website at http://www.geneimprint.com.","authors":[{"name":"Falls J.G."},{"name":"Jirtle R.L."},{"name":"Pulford D.J."},{"name":"Wylie A.A."}],"citationCount":296,"date":"1999-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"American Journal of Pathology","title":"Genomic imprinting: Implications for human disease"},"pmcid":"PMC1866410","pmid":"10079240","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1006/excr.1999.4453","metadata":{"abstract":"Although we inherit two copies of all genes, except those that reside on the sex chromosomes, there is a subset of these genes in which only the paternal or maternal copy is functional. This phenomenon of monoallelic, parent-of-origin expression of genes is termed genomic imprinting. Imprinted genes are normally involved in embryonic growth and behavioral development, but occasionally they also function inappropriately as oncogenes and tumor suppressor genes. The evidence that imprinted genes play a role in carcinogenesis will be discussed in this review. Additional information about imprinted genes can be found on the Genomic Imprinting Website at: (httpd/www.geneimprint.com).","authors":[{"name":"Jirtle R.L."}],"citationCount":109,"date":"1999-04-10T00:00:00Z","journal":"Experimental Cell Research","title":"Genomic imprinting and cancer"},"pmid":"10094809","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Epigenetics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3295"},{"term":"Gene expression","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0203"},{"term":"Gene transcripts","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3512"},{"term":"Human biology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2815"},{"term":"Pathology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0634"},{"term":"Rare diseases","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3325"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"Geneimprint","home":"https://bio.tools/geneimprint","summary":"Catalogue of genes (from different species including human) that contains general information about a gene as well as whether it is imprinted and which allele is expressed.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:geneimprint/web/www.geneimprint.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"me